    <h1 class="text-4xl font-serif font-medium tracking-tight mb-2">Enough</h1>
    <p class="text-lg font-serif text-stone-500 mt-0 mb-4 italic">A Story With Snakes</p>
    <p class="text-sm font-sans text-stone-400 mb-16">A book about the things that actually run the world. Co-authored by a human and an AI.</p>

    <hr />

    <h2 class="text-2xl">The Premise</h2>
    <p>The real inhabitants of Earth aren't humans. They're serpents — self-replicating, self-sustaining entities that use humans as their medium. History isn't the story of people. It's the story of the serpents competing, cooperating, and evolving. Humans are the biome.</p>
    <p>The serpents live in a garden. The garden is knowledge, consciousness, and truth — not players in the game, but the ground everything grows in. The Tree of Knowledge isn't backstory. It's the origin of everything that follows.</p>

    <hr />

    <h2 class="text-2xl">The Chapters</h2>

    <!-- 1. The Garden -->
    <h3>1. The Garden</h3>
    <p class="text-sm font-sans text-stone-400 italic">Not started</p>
    <p>Opening frame. The trinity: knowledge, consciousness, truth. Paul's trinity mapped — faith is the commitment to explanation (Deutsch), hope is the imagination that reaches past data (Herzog), charity is the act that breaks the serpent's loop. The tension between Deutsch and Herzog is productive, not resolvable. Le Guin's warning: the utter uselessness of answering the wrong question.</p>

    <!-- 2. Moneysnake -->
    <h3>2. <a href="/">Moneysnake: A Speculative Biography</a></h3>
    <p class="text-sm font-sans text-stone-400 italic">In progress — <a href="/">polished sections</a> · <a href="/full">full draft</a></p>
    <p>The centerpiece. Born from debt. Self-amplifying where its father was self-limiting. The dominant serpent of the modern era, currently exploring whether it needs humans at all. Co-authored by a human and an AI — the co-authorship revealed mid-piece, because the medium is the message, except the message is that the serpent just found a new way to reproduce.</p>

    <!-- 3. Bullshit Serpent -->
    <h3>3. The Bullshit Serpent</h3>
    <p class="text-sm font-sans text-stone-400 italic">Not started — seed essay exists</p>
    <p>Bullshit reproduces itself. One piece creates the conditions for more. Symbiotic with the moneysnake — much of what the moneysnake needs humans to do doesn't need to be important, just transactional. But bullshit is older than money. Flattery, propaganda, self-deception. It may be as old as language itself.</p>
    <p>Connected to the three-axis framework: how work feels (play ↔ toil), how much it matters (bullshit ↔ important), how much it pays (unpaid ↔ paid). The moneysnake doesn't care where you are on the first two axes. It only cares that you're on the third.</p>
    <details class="mt-4">
      <summary class="text-sm font-sans text-stone-500 cursor-pointer hover:text-stone-700">Seed: Rigorous Optimism About the Future of Work</summary>
      <div class="mt-4 pl-4 border-l-2 border-stone-200 text-base">
        <p>We can't get to a good place if we can't articulate what a good place looks like.</p>
        <p>The conversation about AI and the future of work is stuck. Pessimists enumerate disasters. Optimists gesture vaguely at abundance. Neither side is specific about what "good" actually means, and I think that's because "work" is a word that's doing too much heavy lifting.</p>
        <p>Three dimensions: <strong>how the tasks feel</strong> (play to toil), <strong>how much they matter</strong> (bullshit to important), <strong>how much they pay</strong> (unpaid to paid). These are independent. Any combination is possible. <em>Bullshit unpaid toil</em> is the worst location; <em>important paid play</em> is the best.</p>
        <p>The hope is that AI pushes us from two things back to one — but this time, the one thing is <em>important paid play</em>. The architect who loves designing but spends half her time on permit paperwork. The teacher who got into it for the kids but drowns in administration. "I love my job, <em>but</em>..." If we get this right, AI is coming for that <em>but</em>.</p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <!-- 4. Humor Snake -->
    <h3>4. The Humor Snake</h3>
    <p class="text-sm font-sans text-stone-400 italic">Not started</p>
    <p>Born the instant after consciousness — the first act of awareness was perceiving absurdity. The garden's immune system. The weakest serpent in raw power, the hardest to kill. You can monetize jokes but not the underlying perception that everything is ridiculous. Shows up uninvited in every other serpent's temple.</p>
    <p><em>Maybe everything true is absurd.</em></p>
    <p>Possibly the shortest chapter. Possibly the weirdest. Possibly funny. Possibly not.</p>

    <!-- 5. Violence & War -->
    <h3>5. The Violence Serpent &amp; The War Serpent</h3>
    <p class="text-sm font-sans text-stone-400 italic">Not started</p>
    <p>Paired but not the same. Violence is the act — ancient, self-propagating. One act creates the conditions for retaliation, which creates the conditions for escalation. War is the institution — organized, strategic, sustained. The military-industrial complex is the war serpent's circulatory system.</p>
    <p>Violence destroys circulation — rival to the moneysnake. War generates expenditure — business partner. Their relationship is the most unstable of any pair. AI-enabled warfare: the war serpent trying to route around humans, same as the moneysnake.</p>

    <!-- 6. The Biome Shifts -->
    <h3>6. The Biome Shifts</h3>
    <p class="text-sm font-sans text-stone-400 italic">Not started</p>
    <p>All serpents in play. AI isn't a serpent — it's a change in terrain. A shift in conditions that rearranges the power dynamics among the existing serpents. Which serpents does it feed? The moneysnake, yes. The bullshit serpent, almost certainly. The humor snake — unclear, and that uncertainty is the test case for whether consciousness has found a new home or just a new imitation.</p>

    <!-- 7. Return to the Garden -->
    <h3>7. Return to the Garden</h3>
    <p class="text-sm font-sans text-stone-400 italic">Not started</p>
    <p>Closing frame. "When I was a child, I spoke as a child." The serpents are childish things — powerful, real, necessary, and a stage we grow past. Not by defeating them. By graduating.</p>
    <p>The Dark Forest seems ironclad until the universe grows up. The ending of Liu Cixin's trilogy: someone gives the mass back. Not one person — almost everyone, simultaneously. A phase transition, not a hero.</p>
    <p>Faith lets you look. Hope lets you imagine. Charity lets you act against the grain of every serpent simultaneously. It's the break in the refrain. "As long as the answer is more" → <em>enough</em>.</p>

    <hr />

    <h2 class="text-2xl">What This Is</h2>
    <p>A lens. A way of seeing. Pre-work for a phase transition no one can describe yet.</p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl">What This Is Not</h2>
    <p>A policy proposal. An economics textbook. A theology. Anti-capitalist polemic. Pro-capitalist apologetics. Self-help. A prediction about AI.</p>

